\subsection{Summarizing grades: assignment grades to component grade}%
\label{disjunctmax-summarize}

Now we will describe the [[summarize]] helper function.
We want to establish two things: the most recent date and a suitable grade.

For the most recent date, we just add them to a list as we iterate through the 
submissions.
We do the same for grades, as we iterate through we add any grade to a list.
In the end we compute the maximums of both lists.
The grades we rank are A--E, P and F, plus the borderline [[Fx]].
A submission can arrive with other spellings of those same outcomes:
\enquote{complete}, [[P+]], \enquote{incomplete}, the Canvas label
[[must fix]], or no grade at all.
Only the last two were ever translated here; the first three reached the
ranking untouched and aborted the export with a bare [[KeyError]]
(\cref{p-plus-grades}).
Since that vocabulary is Canvas's and the courses', not this module's, we take
it from [[gradealiases]] (\cref{gradealiases}) rather than keeping a private
copy; a missing submission has the same effect as a failing one, so the
exception path in [[summarize]] records [[F]] directly.

The translation is a good fit here for a reason worth stating.
A disjunctive maximum exists to pick the best of several attempts at the same
examination, so all it needs to know about an attempt is where it ranks.
Neither \enquote{complete} nor [[P+]] says anything more than \enquote{this
attempt passed}, and [[P]] is how a passed attempt is spelled on the scale we
rank---the plus has nowhere to go.
Translating once, before any of the three consumers (the ranking, the summary
and the missing-assignment report) sees the grade, is what keeps them from
drifting apart.

Importing the name at module level also keeps [[normalize_grade]] available as
[[disjunctmax.normalize_grade]], where callers of this module have always
found it.

Surveys follow a rule of their own, which we explain in
\cref{disjunctmax-surveys}: they never enter [[grades]], and an uncompleted
one clears [[surveys_done]].
<<helper functions>>=
def summarize(user, assignments_list):
  """Extracts user's submissions for assignments in assingments_list to 
  summarize results into one grade and a grade date. Summarize by disjunctive 
  maximum; every survey must be completed."""

  grades = []
  dates = []
  graders = []
  surveys_done = True

  for assignment in assignments_list:
    try:
      submission = assignment.get_submission(user,
                                            include=["submission_history"])
    except ResourceDoesNotExist:
      if is_survey(assignment):
        surveys_done = False
      else:
        grades.append("F")
      continue

    submission.assignment = assignment
    graders += results.all_graders(submission)

    if is_survey(assignment):
      surveys_done = surveys_done and survey_completed(submission)
    else:
      grades.append(normalize_grade(submission.grade))

    grade_date = submission.submitted_at or submission.graded_at

    if grade_date:
      grade_date = dt.date.fromisoformat(grade_date.split("T")[0])
      dates.append(grade_date)

  <<combine collected results into [[final_grade]] and [[final_date]]>>

  return (final_grade, final_date, graders)
@

If there are no dates at all, the student hasn't done anything; we return
[[None]] so that the [[results]] command can skip the row entirely.
Otherwise an uncompleted survey caps the grade at F, and only when all
surveys are completed do we award the disjunctive maximum.
A group with no graded assignment at all, only surveys, has nothing to take
the maximum of, so [[grade_max]] returns [[None]] and the grade is F.
<<combine collected results into [[final_grade]] and [[final_date]]>>=
if not dates:
  final_grade = None
  final_date = None
elif not surveys_done:
  final_grade = "F"
  final_date = max(dates)
else:
  final_grade = grade_max(grades) or "F"
  final_date = max(dates)
@

\subsection{Computing the maximum}

To compute the maximum for the A--E grades; we will convert the grades into 
integers, compute the maximum, and convert back.
We also include P/F here, since we can count them as lower than A--E.
<<helper functions>>=
def grade_max(grades):
  """Takes a list of A--E/P--F grades, returns the maximum."""
  num_grades = list(map(grade_to_int, grades))

  if num_grades:
    max_grade = max(num_grades)
    return int_to_grade(max_grade)

  return None

def grade_to_int(grade, assignment=None):
  """Takes a grade, returns its rank among A--E/P/F grades.

  The optional assignment is only used to point at the offending
  submission if the grade isn't one we can rank.
  """
  grade = normalize_grade(grade)
  grade_map = {"F": -2, "Fx": -1,
               "P": 0,
               "E": 1, "D": 2, "C": 3, "B": 4, "A": 5}
  try:
    return grade_map[grade]
  except KeyError:
    <<complain about the unrankable grade>>

def int_to_grade(int_grade):
  grade_map_inv = {-2: "F", -1: "Fx",
                   0: "P",
                   1: "E", 2: "D", 3: "C", 4: "B", 5: "A"}
  return grade_map_inv[int_grade]
@

The module doc promises that we fail if an assignment doesn't use an A--F or
P/F grading scale, and this lookup is where that promise is kept---or was
broken.
A bare [[grade_map[grade]]] raises [[KeyError: 'P+']], which propagates out of
[[summarize]], out of the summary module and out of [[canvaslms results]]:
one unexpected grade on one submission aborts the whole export, and the
traceback names neither the student nor the assignment that caused it.

Now that [[normalize_grade]] handles the aliases we know about, whatever still
reaches this branch is a genuine mismatch between the assignment's grading
scale and this module---a numeric survey score, say.
That deserves an error, but one the teacher can act on: it names the grade, the
assignment it came from when the caller knows it, and the scale we expected.
We suppress the [[KeyError]] context with [[from None]] because the dictionary
lookup is an implementation detail, not information for the reader.

Reporting the assignment is what the optional parameter buys us.
[[missing_assignments]] keeps grades paired with their assignments and passes
it; [[grade_max]] ranks a bare list of grades, so on that path the message
names the grade alone.
<<complain about the unrankable grade>>=
where = f" for assignment {assignment}" if assignment is not None else ""
raise ValueError(
  f"Unknown grade {grade}{where}; disjunctive maximum can only rank "
  f"{', '.join(grade_map)}.") from None
