\subsection{Mandatory surveys}\label{disjunctmax-surveys}

A disjunctive maximum picks the best of several attempts, so most of a group
may be left unattempted.
A survey is different: it is not an attempt at the examination, and
skipping it is not covered by passing another attempt.
The two kinds of assignment therefore combine by two independent rules.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Graded assignments combine by disjunctive maximum: one passing grade
  suffices, the remaining graded assignments may be failed or not attempted
  at all.
\item Every survey is mandatory: it must be completed, and an uncompleted
  survey caps the grade at F.
\end{enumerate}
A survey never enters the maximum.
Its grade carries no information about the student (\cref{surveys}), and
counting even a completed survey as a [[P]] would let a student pass the
group on survey participation alone.
What a survey does contribute is its date, since completing it is part of
finishing the group.

These rules used to live in a separate module, [[maxgradesurvey]], which
recognized surveys by their grading scale; that module is now an alias of
this one (\cref{maxgradesurvey}).
Two of its lessons carry over.
The mandatory-survey rule is tested per \emph{survey}, with [[surveys_done]],
not as \enquote{every assignment has a date}: a graded assignment the
student never attempted has no date either, and the disjunctive rule says
that is fine.
And an uncompleted survey vetoes the grade whether the student has a
submission without any date or no submission at all.

Let's verify the two rules and their interplay on a group of an exam taken
twice and a classic graded survey.
<<test functions>>=
class TestMandatorySurveys:
    """Test that surveys are mandatory and never count towards the max."""

    <<mandatory survey test methods>>
